# Chapter 9 (Isomorphisms) — pain points & missing machinery

A log kept WHILE transliterating Chapter 9, recording what the 2b4m language or
standard library lacked at the moment it blocked a proof. Entries that are ADDRESSED
get removed rather than annotated — what remains is what is still missing. Plain
markdown without a `.md` extension so the literate checker never parses it.

---

## THE REAL LIMIT

**2b4m has no sort of GROUPS.** A group is a theory — a sort plus operations plus axioms — so
`forall G: Group` is unwritable. Identity-by-origin sharpens it: `import group` twice names ONE
theory, so two abstract groups must be DECLARED independently (the choice
`std/group/product.b4m` documents).

**What does NOT follow:** "cannot quantify over theories" does not mean "cannot state theorems
about several theories". It means you must FIX the arity by declaring that many, and declaring
is cheap. Cayley's Theorem needs six declarations (`PermG`, `applyP`, `composeP`, `idP`,
extensionality, `lam`) and then proves — 13 theorems in `std/group/isomorphism.b4m`.
Transitivity of ≅ declares a third group and a second isomorphism, 16 declarations, and the
composite's operation-preservation proves in one `rewrite`.

So Theorem 9.10 is not blocked: `reflexivityOfIsomorphism`, `symmetryOfIsomorphism` and
`transitivityOfIsomorphism` are all proved, plus `compositePreservesIdentity` so the
transitivity witness is an isomorphism of GROUPS and not merely a bijection preserving `op`.

What remains blocked is narrower: the *relation* ≅ cannot be NAMED, because naming it would
quantify over pairs of theories. Every instance you declare is provable; "for all groups G, H"
is not.

**THE RULE:** treat "std has no X" as a fact about a FILE, never about the language. A claim of
"unstatable" requires a failed probe, not a search — and the probe is usually to write the
5–10 lines that declare the structure, then ask what is missing. DECLARE, THEN MODEL: a fresh
sort plus three axioms plus one `model` block inherits an entire corpus.

## STILL MISSING

### `std/function.b4m` is SINGLE-SORTED, so φ : G → H is not an `Fn`

`func apply(f: Fn, x: Element) => Element` (std/function.b4m:34) — a function's source
and target are the SAME sort. An isomorphism's are different by construction, so the
whole `Fn` layer (`invertible`, `inverse`, `compose`, `funcExtensionality`,
`functionFromGraph`) is unavailable to it.

`isomorphism.b4m` therefore declares φ and ψ as primitive functions and states
bijectivity as the two round-trip axioms (`psiAfterPhi`, `phiAfterPsi`), deriving
injectivity and surjectivity from them. That works and is only ~20 lines, but it means
EVERY cross-sort map in std will re-derive the same facts: there is no reusable notion
of "a bijection between two sorts".

What would fix it: a two-sorted function theory (`Map` with `dom`/`cod` sorts as
parameters), of which the current `Fn` is the diagonal case. Not attempted.

### ℤ_n's modulus is a single `const N`, so there is no FAMILY of cyclic groups

`std/integer/mod-n.b4m:49` declares `const N: Int` — one modulus per file. Theorem 9.21
compares ℤ_m × ℤ_n with ℤ_mn for VARYING m and n, which needs three members of a family
indexed by the modulus. `std/integer/mod-n-product.b4m` models ℤ_n × ℤ_n for a single
N, which is the diagonal case.

Memory `zn-via-models-spike` already settled that indexed sorts for ℤ_n are NOT the
answer ("Fixed-const-N QUOTIENT sort + homomorphism axioms"), so the fixed-N design is
deliberate. The cost shows up here: a theorem whose content is a relationship BETWEEN
moduli is out of reach, and §9.2's headline result is exactly that.

### |G| = |H| across an isomorphism needs a listing transported along φ

Theorem 9.6 (2). With counts as listings (§6.2's convention), "|G| = |H|" means: given a
listing of G of length n, φ's image is a listing of H of length n. Reachable — φ is
injective and surjective, which is what a listing transport needs — but not built, and
it would want `anyTwoGroupListingsHaveTheSameLength` on the H side to make the length
well-defined. Theorem 9.6 (4) and (5) (cyclic transfers, subgroup orders transfer) are
the same shape: the machinery exists over ONE group and does not travel along φ.

### The order of a tuple is the lcm — needs `power` over the product sort

Theorem 9.17. `pairIsIdentityIffComponentsAre` (added to `std/group/product.b4m`) is the
structural half: the exponents killing (g,h) are the common multiples of the component
orders. Getting to "the order IS the lcm" needs `power` over `Pair` — which
`model ProductGroup` makes available in principle, since `std/group/power.b4m` is a
group theory — plus lcm minimality from `std/integer/lattice.b4m`. Neither connection is
made. `cubeIsIdentityComponentwise` is the k = 3 instance someone wrote by hand, which
is the tell that the general version was wanted and skipped.

## WHAT WORKED, worth keeping

### Declaring two groups and modelling each onto `std/group.b4m` costs nothing

`isomorphism.b4m` declares G and H with three axioms each, then
`model GIsAGroup` / `model HIsAGroup` make both real groups — so right identity, right
inverse, cancellation and identity-uniqueness all transfer instead of being re-proved
twice. `phiPreservesIdentity` uses H's `cancelRight` through the model in one line.
Six axioms of boilerplate for two full group corpora.

A gotcha inside that: a model-transferred theorem keeps the SOURCE's binder order.
`group.cancelRight` is `forall b, a, c` with the shared factor as `a`, and writing the
transferred statement as `forall a, b, c; opH(a,c) = opH(b,c) -> a = b` fails with a
good diagnostic naming the derived form. Read the source statement, do not guess.

### `--library` caught a real gap in the FIRST draft of this file

The §9.1 theorems all reason in H, so the first version of `isomorphism.b4m` never used
`opGAssoc` or `opGInverseLeft` — G's structure was declared but idle, and
`check --library std` failed with "2 unused axiom(s)". Adding Cayley's Theorem fixed it
genuinely rather than by adding a token example: `leftTranslationIsSurjective` needs the
inverse, and `leftTranslationComposes` IS associativity. The gate turned "I should
probably do Cayley" into "the library is inconsistent until I do", which is the right
pressure.

