## Mechanical frictions worth knowing

- a DEFINITION BLOCK cannot infer a clause variable's sort when it appears only inside an
  existential: `pred inImage(s: PermG): inImage(s) iff (exists g: G; s = lam(g))` fails with
  "cannot infer the sort of 'g'". A declared `pred` plus a named `axiom` works, and is what a
  model would need anyway (a definition clause's name is mangled).
- `specialize` on a `forall`-quantified AXIOM treats the binder as a premise to discharge
  ("cannot discharge the guard premise"). `apply_at` is the right rule.
- an `iff` axiom needs explicit elimination before either direction is usable —
  `[using tautology <hyp> <the-iff-at-the-point>]`, not a direct cite.
- a model-transferred theorem keeps the SOURCE's binder order. `group.cancelRight` is
  `forall b, a, c` with the shared factor as `a`; writing the transferred statement as
  `forall a, b, c` fails. Read the source statement, do not guess.

### THEOREM NUMBERS: cite by content, not by a derived number

PreTeXt auto-numbers, so no number appears in the XML, and DERIVING one by counting
`<theorem|proposition|corollary|lemma|example|definition>` blocks is unreliable three ways:
numbering is per CHAPTER not per section; some blocks have no `xml:id` at all, so a regex keyed
on it silently shifts every later number; and `<example>`s share the sequence, so they must be
counted but not cited. Chapter 6's eleven citations were all wrong from this, and two results
cannot be numbered at all because their blocks have no id.

RULE: cite a numbered result by its STATEMENT unless the number is verified against the
rendered book. A derived number is a guess that looks like a fact.
